%!testlib
%!
% Every filter that can round-trip, asked the same question about where
% it stops.
%
% A decoding filter reads until its end-of-data and no further. What it
% must not do is read further and throw the excess away, because the
% bytes after the end-of-data belong to whoever holds the underlying
% file, and a program that puts a filter over part of a stream and then
% reads the rest itself is the ordinary case -- it is how a font program
% is read out of the middle of a document.
%
% That is not hypothetical here. The last defect in these filters was a
% lazy end-of-data desynchronisation, and it was in ALL FIVE stream
% decoders at once, because each was written by copying the one before
% it. A question asked of one member and not the others is exactly how a
% family comes to share a defect.
%
% So it is asked of every member that can be asked. A round trip needs
% both halves, so the members here are the six with an encoder and a
% decoder both; SubFileDecode and ReusableStreamDecode have no encoder
% to build their input with, and DCT is lossy and would not round-trip a
% string. Those three are covered by tests/check-filter-facts.sh, which
% asks the whole family of seventeen what each one is.
%
% The shape of each case is the same:
%
%   encode a known string into a file, and read that file back as bytes
%   write those bytes to a second file, followed by a sentinel
%   decode the second file: the decoding must give the known string
%   then read the underlying file: what is left must be the sentinel
%
% The last line is the one that matters. A decoder that consumed the
% sentinel passed the first three and would have passed a test that
% stopped there.


/PLAIN (HELLO FILTER WORLD) def
/SENTINEL (ZZ) def
/TMPA (zzfilter-a.tmp) def
/TMPB (zzfilter-b.tmp) def

% { proc }  outcome  /ok | /errorname

% (path)  slurp  (contents)
% reads a whole file, in pieces, so no case depends on a buffer size
/slurp {                                % (path)  .  (contents)
    (r) file
    /acc () def
    { dup 512 string readstring
      exch /chunk exch def
      /acc acc chunk concatstrings def
      not { exit } if
    } loop
    closefile
    acc
} bind def

/concatstrings {                        % (a) (b)  .  (ab)
    /csb exch def
    /csa exch def
    csa length csb length add string
    dup 0 csa putinterval
    dup csa length csb putinterval
} bind def

% /Name  encoded  (bytes the encoder wrote for PLAIN)
/encoded {                              % /Name  .  (bytes)
    /enm exch def
    TMPA (w) file
    dup enm filter
    dup PLAIN writestring
    closefile                           % the filter: writes its EOD
    closefile                           % the file underneath it
    TMPA slurp
} bind def

% A member is a name and the sample it is asked to carry. Most take any
% bytes at all. CCITTFax does not: it encodes bilevel scanlines of a
% declared width, and with the default 1728 columns one row is 216
% bytes, so it is given exactly that rather than left out of the
% question every other member answers. The pattern is varied, so a
% decoder returning a constant would not pass.
/ROW 216 string def
0 1 215 { /i exch def ROW i i 7 mul 31 add 255 and put } for

% The third entry is what the member is held to on the last question.
% /strict is the answer every member should give, and every member gives
% it: each decoder stops at its own end-of-data and leaves the bytes after
% it for whoever reads next. The column stays because it is what let the
% one member that did not be held to the wrong answer on purpose, so the
% note could not outlive the defect -- and it did not: the day
% CCITTFaxDecode stopped eating the sentinel, this line failed and asked
% to be made strict.
/members [
    (ASCIIHex)  PLAIN /strict
    (ASCII85)   PLAIN /strict
    (RunLength) PLAIN /strict
    (LZW)       PLAIN /strict
    (Flate)     PLAIN /strict
    (CCITTFax)  ROW   /strict
] def

0 3 members length 3 sub {
    /mi exch def
    /base    members mi get def
    /PLAIN   members mi 1 add get def
    /holdas  members mi 2 add get def
    /encname base (Encode) concatstrings cvn def
    /decname base (Decode) concatstrings cvn def

    % ---- can this build encode at all? A filter absent from the build
    %      answers undefined, and a case that cannot run must say so
    %      rather than pass.
    /enc null def
    { /enc encname encoded def } outcome /ok eq
    dup not {
        base (is in this build) assert2
    }{ pop
        % ---- lay the encoded bytes down followed by the sentinel
        TMPB (w) file
        dup enc writestring
        dup SENTINEL writestring
        closefile

        % ---- decode, and read the rest of the file afterwards
        /got null def
        /rest null def
        { TMPB (r) file
          /basefile exch def
          basefile decname filter
          /df exch def
          /acc () def
          { df 512 string readstring
            exch /chunk exch def
            /acc acc chunk concatstrings def
            not { exit } if
          } loop
          /got acc def
          basefile 64 string readstring pop
          /rest exch def
          basefile closefile
        } outcome /ok eq
        dup not {
            base (round trips without raising) assert2
        }{ pop
            got PLAIN eq
            base (decodes to what was encoded) assert2

            holdas /strict eq {
                rest SENTINEL eq
                base (leaves the bytes after its end-of-data for the next reader) assert2
            }{
                rest SENTINEL ne
                base (still eats the bytes after its end-of-data -- if this line failed, the defect in task 330 is fixed and the member is now /strict) assert2
            } ifelse
        } ifelse
    } ifelse
} for

% A reusable stream names the filters to run its source through, and the
% parameters those filters take come with them in DecodeParms (PLRM
% 3.13.3): one dictionary against a lone name, an array running alongside
% an array of them. SubFileDecode can be reached no other way here, having
% no operands of its own in a chain to read its end-of-data from -- PLRM
% 3.13.2 says its parameters "must be specified in the parameter
% dictionary if the SubFileDecode filter is used as one of the filters in
% a ReusableStreamDecode filter".
{ (abcdef@gh) << /Filter /SubFileDecode
                 /DecodeParms << /EODCount 0 /EODString (@) >> >>
  /ReusableStreamDecode filter 20 string readstring pop (abcdef) eq } outcome
    dup /ok eq { pop true } { pop false } ifelse
(ReusableStreamDecode) (gives a SubFileDecode in its chain the end-of-data DecodeParms names) assert2

% the same filter reached through an array of one, with its parameters in
% the array running alongside
{ (abcdef@gh) << /Filter [ /SubFileDecode ]
                 /DecodeParms [ << /EODCount 0 /EODString (@) >> ] >>
  /ReusableStreamDecode filter 20 string readstring pop (abcdef) eq } outcome
    dup /ok eq { pop true } { pop false } ifelse
(ReusableStreamDecode) (takes those parameters from an array beside an array of filters) assert2

% PLRM 3.13 Table 3.23 marks EODCount and EODString both Required of the
% parameter dictionary. A dictionary carrying only the string is taken as
% asking for a count of zero, which ends the stream at the first
% occurrence of it; COMPLIANCE records the leniency.
{ (abcdef@gh) << /Filter /SubFileDecode
                 /DecodeParms << /EODString (@) >> >>
  /ReusableStreamDecode filter 20 string readstring pop (abcdef) eq } outcome
    dup /ok eq { pop true } { pop false } ifelse
(ReusableStreamDecode) (takes a count of zero where the parameters name only the string) assert2

% and the count so defaulted is zero rather than a licence to read on: a
% second occurrence of the string is past the end, not part of the stream
{ (abcdef@gh@ij) << /EODString (@) >> /SubFileDecode filter
  20 string readstring pop (abcdef) eq } outcome
    dup /ok eq { pop true } { pop false } ifelse
(SubFileDecode) (ends at the first occurrence where the count was defaulted) assert2

% the string is not defaulted with it: without one there is no condition
% to recognise at all. An absent required entry is an undefined error --
% the rule PLRM 8.2 states for a dictionary-parameterised operator under
% sethalftone, and the answer get gives for a key a dictionary does not
% hold -- and undefined is among the errors filter is defined to raise.
{ (abcdef@gh) << /EODCount 0 >> /SubFileDecode filter
  20 string readstring pop pop } outcome
    dup /ok eq { pop false } { pop true } ifelse
(SubFileDecode) (refuses a parameter dictionary that names no end-of-data string) assert2

% a filter that takes no parameters is still built where the chain names
% none for it
{ (414243>) << /Filter /ASCIIHexDecode >> /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  10 string readstring pop (ABC) eq } outcome
    dup /ok eq { pop true } { pop false } ifelse
(ReusableStreamDecode) (still builds a filter the chain gives no parameters) assert2

% A reusable stream is read to its end before it answers anything, so its
% whole source becomes a buffer the process holds -- and that buffer is not
% in VM, so nothing the language offers bounds it. Left unbounded it is an
% amplifier and not merely a cost: the byte 0x81 is a fixed point of
% run-length decoding, so a stream of them grows sixty-four times for every
% decoding layer stacked over it, and a program of a couple of hundred
% bytes reaches hundreds of gigabytes in six. What is asked here is that
% the stated limit is reached and refused, and that a stream below it is
% carried as before.
/rf.seed (\201\201) def
{ rf.seed
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  closefile } outcome /ok eq
    (four layers of the amplification are carried) assert
{ rf.seed
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  closefile } outcome /ok ne
    (a fifth is refused rather than held) assert

% A filter that cannot be built says why. The reasons are different and
% PLRM names them differently: an implementation limit is a limitcheck
% (PLRM 3.11), a parameter outside what the entry admits is a rangecheck,
% and memory run out is a VMerror. They all used to arrive as ioerror,
% because a constructor answered with an object and an object has no room
% for a reason.
{ rf.seed
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  /RunLengthDecode filter /ReusableStreamDecode filter
  closefile } outcome /limitcheck eq
    (a reusable stream past its size is a limitcheck) assert
{ (x) << /Predictor 2 /Colors 0 >> /FlateDecode filter } outcome /rangecheck eq
    (a predictor parameter outside its range is a rangecheck) assert
{ (x) 0 (0123456789012345678901234567890123456789012345678901234567890123456789)
  /SubFileDecode filter } outcome /limitcheck eq
    (an end-of-data marker longer than the filter carries is a limitcheck) assert

verdict
